            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Rosa's Role and Sacrifices</strong><br>\nRosa's act of defiance and the subsequent bus boycott made her a symbol of the struggle for civil rights. However, her activism came with a personal cost. She lost her job as a seamstress and received numerous threats to her life. Despite these challenges, she remained a committed activist for racial equality.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Later Years and Advocacy</strong><br>\nAfter the boycott, Rosa moved to Detroit, where she continued to be an advocate for justice and equality. She worked for Congressman John Conyers and founded the Rosa and Raymond Parks Institute for Self Development. Through her later years, she focused on improving the lives of African Americans and young people.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Recognition and Honors</strong><br>\nRosa Parks received many accolades during her lifetime, including the Presidential Medal of Freedom and the Congressional Gold Medal. Her arrest on the bus and her role in the civil rights movement made her an icon of resistance to racial segregation and social inequality.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
